1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:01,466
Look at this cup.

2
00:00:01,466 --> 00:00:04,733
It is sixteen hundred years old, and it is green.

3
00:00:04,733 --> 00:00:06,333
Dull, milky, jade green.

4
00:00:06,799 --> 00:00:08,913
Now move the light behind it.

5
00:00:08,913 --> 00:00:09,569
Same cup.

6
00:00:09,569 --> 00:00:10,371
Same glass.

7
00:00:10,371 --> 00:00:11,099
Blood red.

8
00:00:11,566 --> 00:00:14,304
For years, people called it impossible.

9
00:00:14,304 --> 00:00:23,500
Some still say the Romans had a lost science, or a gift from somewhere else, because the trick inside this glass is nanotechnology.

10
00:00:23,966 --> 00:00:28,146
So tonight we examine it the way the cup asks to be examined.

11
00:00:28,146 --> 00:00:28,557
Twice.

12
00:00:28,557 --> 00:00:31,161
In front light, the story people tell.

13
00:00:31,161 --> 00:00:33,833
In back light, what the evidence shows.

14
00:00:34,299 --> 00:00:35,398
Nine questions.

15
00:00:35,398 --> 00:00:36,277
A scorecard.

16
00:00:36,277 --> 00:00:39,500
And a verdict on the Roman nanotech mystery.

17
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:40,233
Lights on.

18
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:45,607
Question one.

19
00:00:45,607 --> 00:00:46,933
What exactly is it?

20
00:00:46,933 --> 00:00:47,771
Front light.

21
00:00:47,771 --> 00:00:55,099
It sits in the British Museum, in Room forty-one, registered as nineteen fifty-eight, twelve oh two, one.

22
00:00:55,566 --> 00:01:02,386
It is about sixteen centimetres tall and thirteen wide, and it weighs around seven hundred grams.

23
00:01:02,386 --> 00:01:09,066
It was made in the fourth century A D, probably between about two ninety and three twenty-five.

24
00:01:09,533 --> 00:01:11,829
It is a cage cup, a diatretum.

25
00:01:11,829 --> 00:01:14,277
The decoration was not stuck on.

26
00:01:14,277 --> 00:01:23,766
It was cut and ground out of one thick block of glass, until the figures stood free, held to the wall by tiny glass bridges.

27
00:01:24,233 --> 00:01:24,991
Back light.

28
00:01:24,991 --> 00:01:27,060
The colour is the real puzzle.

29
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:29,680
In reflected light it looks like jade.

30
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:33,610
With light passing through it, it turns translucent ruby.

31
00:01:33,610 --> 00:01:37,333
Glass that does this is called dichroic, two-coloured.

32
00:01:37,799 --> 00:01:44,505
A handful of other Roman glasses change colour, but only as fragments, and none as strongly.

33
00:01:44,505 --> 00:01:48,733
This is the only complete Roman object made of this glass.

34
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:54,911
One historian called it the most spectacular glass of the period that we know existed.

35
00:01:54,911 --> 00:01:57,966
So who made it, and what does its picture say?

36
00:01:58,433 --> 00:01:59,444
Question two.

37
00:01:59,444 --> 00:02:02,400
Front light: the story on the surface.

38
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:09,322
This is Lycurgus, a mythical king of Thrace, from a legend already told in Homer's Iliad.

39
00:02:09,322 --> 00:02:13,833
He attacked Ambrosia, a follower of the wine god Dionysus.

40
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:20,906
She called on Mother Earth and became a vine, and the vine coiled around the king and held him.

41
00:02:20,906 --> 00:02:26,400
On the cup, Dionysus, his panther, Pan and a satyr with a rock close in on him.

42
00:02:26,866 --> 00:02:30,088
And here is a detail you only see in back light.

43
00:02:30,088 --> 00:02:36,197
The king's body is cut from a patch of glass with a slightly different colour, more violet.

44
00:02:36,197 --> 00:02:41,500
The carver used it, so the king's rage glows hotter than everything around him.

45
00:02:41,966 --> 00:02:50,956
Some scholars think the scene was political, a nod to the emperor Constantine defeating his rival Licinius in three twenty-four.

46
00:02:50,956 --> 00:02:57,066
Others think green turning red meant grapes ripening, a cup for feasts of the wine god.

47
00:02:57,533 --> 00:02:59,518
Its early history is blank.

48
00:02:59,518 --> 00:03:03,120
It first appears in print in eighteen forty-five.

49
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:11,500
The Rothschild family owned it by eighteen fifty-seven, when a visiting art expert called it barbaric and debased.

50
00:03:11,966 --> 00:03:19,675
In nineteen fifty-eight Victor, Lord Rothschild, sold it to the British Museum for twenty thousand pounds.

51
00:03:19,675 --> 00:03:22,657
By then, experts had a stranger question.

52
00:03:22,657 --> 00:03:23,966
Was it even glass?

53
00:03:24,433 --> 00:03:25,467
Question three.

54
00:03:25,467 --> 00:03:27,743
Front light: it looks like stone.

55
00:03:27,743 --> 00:03:34,433
Until the first full study in nineteen fifty, nobody was certain it was glass and not a gemstone.

56
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:37,672
It really does resemble jade, or opal.

57
00:03:37,672 --> 00:03:46,500
Romans prized cups carved from semi-precious stone, and the finest glasscutters borrowed their skills from gem engravers.

58
00:03:46,966 --> 00:03:47,781
Back light.

59
00:03:47,781 --> 00:03:55,490
A mineralogist at the Natural History Museum, F A Claringbull, concluded it was glass, not opal or jade.

60
00:03:55,490 --> 00:03:59,566
In nineteen fifty-nine, X-ray diffraction confirmed it.

61
00:04:00,033 --> 00:04:07,076
The same year, a tiny sample went to the research labs of the General Electric Company in Wembley.

62
00:04:07,076 --> 00:04:10,166
The answer came back surprisingly ordinary.

63
00:04:10,633 --> 00:04:12,363
Soda-lime-silica glass.

64
00:04:12,363 --> 00:04:18,157
The same basic recipe as most Roman glass, and as your window or your bottle.

65
00:04:18,157 --> 00:04:20,715
So the magic was not in the glass.

66
00:04:20,715 --> 00:04:23,500
It was in something hidden inside it.

67
00:04:23,966 --> 00:04:24,954
Question four.

68
00:04:24,954 --> 00:04:26,506
What is hiding inside?

69
00:04:26,506 --> 00:04:34,266
Front light: the popular version says the Romans ground gold and silver into dust and mixed it into the glass.

70
00:04:34,733 --> 00:04:35,521
Back light.

71
00:04:35,521 --> 00:04:46,633
In the nineteen sixties, Robert Brill of the Corning Museum of Glass, working with the G E C team, analysed the cup and made experimental melts of his own.

72
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:52,268
The colour was linked to gold and silver, but in astonishingly tiny amounts.

73
00:04:52,268 --> 00:04:57,233
About forty parts per million of gold, and about three hundred of silver.

74
00:04:57,699 --> 00:05:03,210
That is roughly fifteen grams of gold spread through a bathtub full of glass.

75
00:05:03,210 --> 00:05:06,000
No Roman could weigh that out directly.

76
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:13,300
More likely, a larger dose was added to a big melt, and then diluted again and again with plain glass.

77
00:05:13,766 --> 00:05:16,161
And adding the metals was not enough.

78
00:05:16,161 --> 00:05:24,833
Brill found the glass also had to be heat treated, under the right chemical conditions, so the metal could gather into tiny particles.

79
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:27,071
Too little heat, no colour.

80
00:05:27,071 --> 00:05:28,843
Too much, the wrong colour.

81
00:05:28,843 --> 00:05:33,633
The result depends on temperature, time, and even the air in the furnace.

82
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:36,527
But nobody had seen the particles yet.

83
00:05:36,527 --> 00:05:39,722
Their instruments could not prove they were there.

84
00:05:39,722 --> 00:05:43,300
That took another twenty years, and a beam of electrons.

85
00:05:43,766 --> 00:05:44,684
Question five.

86
00:05:44,684 --> 00:05:46,782
What do the particles look like?

87
00:05:46,782 --> 00:05:55,566
In the late nineteen eighties, David Barber and Ian Freestone put a tiny fragment of the cup under a transmission electron microscope.

88
00:05:56,033 --> 00:05:57,443
And there they were.

89
00:05:57,443 --> 00:06:01,672
Specks of metal, typically around seventy nanometres across.

90
00:06:01,672 --> 00:06:04,633
Far too small for any ordinary microscope.

91
00:06:05,100 --> 00:06:07,925
Some reports put them as small as fifty.

92
00:06:07,925 --> 00:06:12,800
Either way, less than a thousandth the size of a grain of table salt.

93
00:06:13,266 --> 00:06:14,943
They are not pure gold.

94
00:06:14,943 --> 00:06:21,940
They are an alloy, about seven parts silver to three parts gold, with around ten percent copper.

95
00:06:21,940 --> 00:06:25,366
Alongside them, tiny crystals of ordinary salt.

96
00:06:25,833 --> 00:06:28,552
At that size, something strange happens.

97
00:06:28,552 --> 00:06:32,019
The particles are close to the wavelength of light.

98
00:06:32,019 --> 00:06:36,166
Their electrons ripple together, a surface plasmon resonance.

99
00:06:36,633 --> 00:06:42,963
Shine light through, and the particles soak up and scatter blue and green, so only red gets out.

100
00:06:42,963 --> 00:06:47,248
Bounce light off, and the scattered green comes back to your eye.

101
00:06:47,248 --> 00:06:48,633
One cup, two colours.

102
00:06:49,100 --> 00:06:51,037
So the mechanism is solved.

103
00:06:51,037 --> 00:06:53,550
Real nanoparticles, in a Roman cup.

104
00:06:53,550 --> 00:06:57,282
That is the fact that launched a thousand headlines.

105
00:06:57,282 --> 00:06:59,866
But how did the cup itself get made?

106
00:07:00,333 --> 00:07:01,229
Question six.

107
00:07:01,229 --> 00:07:05,713
Front light: some claim the cup is too perfect for ancient tools.

108
00:07:05,713 --> 00:07:06,472
Back light.

109
00:07:06,472 --> 00:07:09,300
The British Museum looked for tool marks.

110
00:07:09,766 --> 00:07:15,494
When the metal foot was removed, a loose fragment of the original openwork was found.

111
00:07:15,494 --> 00:07:22,166
Under the microscope, it carries faint marks of grinding and polishing everywhere, even underneath.

112
00:07:22,633 --> 00:07:31,297
Crescent-shaped cuts point to rotating wheels of copper, bronze or iron, fed with a gritty slurry of quartz or emery.

113
00:07:31,297 --> 00:07:36,333
Pliny even mentions slivers of diamond used for cutting hard stones.

114
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:39,620
It began as a heavy, thick-walled blank.

115
00:07:39,620 --> 00:07:44,979
In the nineteen sixties, the Corning Glass Works even made a replica of one.

116
00:07:44,979 --> 00:07:47,800
Then a master cutter removed most of it.

117
00:07:48,266 --> 00:07:53,679
Even with modern power tools, a cage cup like this takes a great deal of time.

118
00:07:53,679 --> 00:08:00,133
Behind the figures, the inside was hollowed out too, so the light passes through them evenly.

119
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:03,623
It may have passed through three workshops.

120
00:08:03,623 --> 00:08:10,161
Raw glass from Egypt or Palestine, a specialist who made the coloured blank, and the cutters.

121
00:08:10,161 --> 00:08:10,934
Skill, yes.

122
00:08:10,934 --> 00:08:12,199
Mystery tools, no.

123
00:08:12,666 --> 00:08:13,606
Question seven.

124
00:08:13,606 --> 00:08:15,487
This is the heart of the case.

125
00:08:15,487 --> 00:08:18,056
Did the Romans know what they were doing?

126
00:08:18,056 --> 00:08:21,566
Front light: they must have, the mixture is too precise.

127
00:08:22,033 --> 00:08:22,849
Back light.

128
00:08:22,849 --> 00:08:27,227
The few other Roman dichroic pieces are all over the place.

129
00:08:27,227 --> 00:08:31,159
One fragment turns from olive green to reddish amber.

130
00:08:31,159 --> 00:08:35,166
Another from buff to brown, coloured mostly by silver.

131
00:08:35,633 --> 00:08:39,700
Freestone and his colleagues called the process hit and miss.

132
00:08:39,700 --> 00:08:43,166
The makers may not even have known gold was the key.

133
00:08:43,166 --> 00:08:49,766
It could have come in as a little gold in silver, or from scraps of gold leaf left in the workshop.

134
00:08:50,233 --> 00:08:56,998
The Sheffield team's new replication found the colour shifts with where the glass sits in the furnace.

135
00:08:56,998 --> 00:09:00,712
Freestone's verdict: the Romans could hardly control it.

136
00:09:00,712 --> 00:09:03,100
With this cup, they hit the jackpot.

137
00:09:03,566 --> 00:09:04,861
Yet they valued it.

138
00:09:04,861 --> 00:09:15,766
A letter said to be from the emperor Hadrian mentions parti-coloured cups that change colour, a gift from a temple priest, to be used at banquets on feast days.

139
00:09:16,233 --> 00:09:17,582
And then it stopped.

140
00:09:17,582 --> 00:09:21,563
The trick seems to have been used for only about a century.

141
00:09:21,563 --> 00:09:30,133
Making red glass with gold did not become routine until the seventeenth century, credited to the German chemist Johann Kunckel.

142
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:36,771
A recipe nobody understood, controlled by nobody, lost within a few generations.

143
00:09:36,771 --> 00:09:40,397
That is not how a secret super-science behaves.

144
00:09:40,397 --> 00:09:42,866
It is how a lucky craft behaves.

145
00:09:43,333 --> 00:09:44,357
Question eight.

146
00:09:44,357 --> 00:09:46,065
Has anyone done it again?

147
00:09:46,065 --> 00:09:55,833
In eighteen fifty-six, Michael Faraday, in the basement of the Royal Institution, accidentally made a ruby red liquid while thinning gold leaf.

148
00:09:56,299 --> 00:10:00,325
He realised it was full of gold particles too small to see.

149
00:10:00,325 --> 00:10:09,399
Some of his bottles still survive, and a laser through them still shows his cone of light, more than a hundred and fifty years later.

150
00:10:09,866 --> 00:10:21,727
In twenty thirteen, Gang Logan Liu's team at the University of Illinois coated billions of tiny wells on a plastic chip with gold or silver nanoparticles.

151
00:10:21,727 --> 00:10:24,500
Billions of miniature Lycurgus cups.

152
00:10:24,966 --> 00:10:28,891
Fill them with different liquids and the colour changes.

153
00:10:28,891 --> 00:10:30,223
Water, light green.

154
00:10:30,223 --> 00:10:30,854
Oil, red.

155
00:10:30,854 --> 00:10:37,233
Their prototype was a hundred times more sensitive to salt than similar commercial sensors.

156
00:10:37,700 --> 00:10:48,700
In twenty twenty, Dutch researchers made a printable plastic that switches green to red the same way, in what they called the quest for 3D printing the Lycurgus cup.

157
00:10:49,166 --> 00:11:00,133
And in October twenty twenty-six, the University of Sheffield announced it had recreated the effect in glass, using a recipe derived from a Roman fragment.

158
00:11:00,133 --> 00:11:04,166
A glass artist, Elliot Walker, built a sculpture from it.

159
00:11:04,633 --> 00:11:10,598
Freestone called it more ambitious and more precise than the nineteen sixties attempt.

160
00:11:10,598 --> 00:11:14,551
The technician who made it, Lisa Hollands, put it simply.

161
00:11:14,551 --> 00:11:15,799
To me, it's magic.

162
00:11:16,266 --> 00:11:17,276
Question nine.

163
00:11:17,276 --> 00:11:18,647
The alien question.

164
00:11:18,647 --> 00:11:29,399
The claim: a fourth-century cup using nanotechnology is out of place, so the knowledge must have come from visitors, or a lost advanced civilisation.

165
00:11:29,866 --> 00:11:31,915
Online, the story has grown.

166
00:11:31,915 --> 00:11:36,526
Viral posts call it Greek, from Sparta, with mysterious powers.

167
00:11:36,526 --> 00:11:43,333
A fact check by A F P answered: it is not from Sparta, and the technology has been explained.

168
00:11:43,799 --> 00:11:46,913
Test the alien idea against the evidence.

169
00:11:46,913 --> 00:11:47,824
Known glass.

170
00:11:47,824 --> 00:11:48,963
Known furnaces.

171
00:11:48,963 --> 00:11:49,950
Known wheels.

172
00:11:49,950 --> 00:11:53,747
Results that varied piece to piece, then vanished.

173
00:11:53,747 --> 00:11:56,633
Advanced visitors would not need luck.

174
00:11:57,100 --> 00:11:57,890
The verdict.

175
00:11:57,890 --> 00:11:59,933
Time to score the Lycurgus cup.

176
00:12:00,399 --> 00:12:03,896
Real Roman glass, fourth century: evidence.

177
00:12:03,896 --> 00:12:07,717
Gold and silver nanoparticles inside: evidence.

178
00:12:07,717 --> 00:12:11,051
Cut by hand on lapidary wheels: evidence.

179
00:12:11,051 --> 00:12:14,466
A deliberate, understood recipe: unlikely.

180
00:12:14,933 --> 00:12:19,073
Hit-and-miss process, lost after a century: likely.

181
00:12:19,073 --> 00:12:23,294
A real Roman nanotechnology, by result: partly true.

182
00:12:23,294 --> 00:12:26,866
Alien or lost advanced science: no evidence.

183
00:12:27,333 --> 00:12:30,277
Case closed, and the cup keeps its glow.

184
00:12:30,277 --> 00:12:31,823
Roman nanotechnology?

185
00:12:31,823 --> 00:12:32,927
By result, yes.

186
00:12:32,927 --> 00:12:34,473
By understanding, no.

187
00:12:34,473 --> 00:12:43,233
Human glassmakers made particles they could never see, and a master cutter turned their lucky batch into a masterpiece.

188
00:12:43,700 --> 00:12:50,156
Somewhere, sixteen centuries ago, someone held this glass up to a lamp and watched it turn red.

189
00:12:50,156 --> 00:12:51,651
They did not know why.

190
00:12:51,651 --> 00:12:54,166
We only found out in nineteen ninety.

191
00:12:54,633 --> 00:12:59,311
Next case: an Egyptian temple wall that seems to show a helicopter.

192
00:12:59,311 --> 00:13:02,733
Carved three thousand years ago, or carved twice?

193
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:05,900
Green or red, which story do you believe?

194
00:13:05,900 --> 00:13:11,433
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
